====================================================================================================
S2. VALIDATION -- v_rms(z=0) from the same integral, against the observed ~300 km/s
====================================================================================================
      linear: INT P dk =    4460.89 Mpc^2  ->  v_rms 3D =  536.7 km/s, 1D =  309.9 km/s
   nonlinear: INT P dk =    6548.17 Mpc^2  ->  v_rms 3D =  650.3 km/s, 1D =  375.4 km/s
  CORRECTION TO MY OWN VALIDATION TARGET: the integral gives the 3D rms; the familiar
  ~300 km/s figure is the 1D (line-of-sight) value, so the like-for-like comparison is
  v_1D = v_3D/sqrt(3). Comparing 3D to 1D was my error, not a code error.
  [OK] linear v_rms(1D, z=0) = 310 km/s against the observed ~300 km/s -- INT P dk is validated against a known quantity

====================================================================================================
S3. THE UV WORRY, SETTLED BY COMPUTATION -- contribution to <g^2> per ln k
====================================================================================================
  d<g^2>/dln k  proportional to  k P(k). If that peaks at small k the integral is
  large-scale dominated and well defined; if it rises to the resolution limit it is
  cutoff-dominated and the answer is NOT determined. Computed at z = 2.85:
     k (1/Mpc)  lambda (Mpc)  k P(k) (norm)
        0.0030       2104.78         0.0900
        0.0101        625.11         0.5396
        0.0299        209.94         0.9011
        0.0992         63.32         0.7219
        0.3001         20.94         0.3063
        0.9949          6.32         0.1255
        3.0082          2.09         0.0852
        9.9744          0.63         0.0428
       30.1593          0.21         0.0128
  PEAK at k = 0.0482 1/Mpc, i.e. lambda = 130.4 Mpc
  cumulative fraction of INT P dk below k_max:
      k <    0.1 1/Mpc :  0.696
      k <    0.3 1/Mpc :  0.859
      k <    1.0 1/Mpc :  0.931
      k <    3.0 1/Mpc :  0.965
      k <   10.0 1/Mpc :  0.989
      k <   30.0 1/Mpc :  0.998
      k <  100.0 1/Mpc :  1.000
  [OK] 93% of the integral comes from k < 1/Mpc, so <g^2> is LARGE-SCALE dominated and NOT cutoff-sensitive -- the risk I flagged does not materialise
  So the total acceleration is set by ~10-100 Mpc structure, not by unresolved small scales.
  IMPORTANT CONSEQUENCE: it does NOT get a big boost from embedded small-scale structure,
  which is what the forest result needed in order to be rescued.

