====================================================================================================
S5. The forest exclusion re-run on x_rms (f_pec = 0.3, T = 1e4 K, conservative sqrt scaling)
====================================================================================================
      z     x_rms  sqrt(1/h)  b_cut pred  b_cut obs    sigma
   2.30   0.04914       3.20        41.4       24.0     8.7s
   2.85   0.05677       2.98        35.2       22.0     6.6s
   3.35   0.06368       2.81        23.4       17.0     3.2s
   3.70   0.06852       2.71        18.4       15.0     1.7s
  exclusion range on x_rms: 1.7 - 8.7 sigma
  (for comparison, the single-absorber estimate gave 6.5 - 42.8 sigma on the same footing)
  [OK] MIXED, and this is the honest outcome: the exclusion SOFTENS from 6.5-42.8 sigma to 1.7-8.7 sigma. It survives at low z (z<=2.85: 6.6-8.7 sigma) but DIES at z=3.7. The escape partially works

====================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  1. THE ESCAPE PARTIALLY WORKS. Computing the TOTAL acceleration from the real P(k) raises
     forest gas from x = 0.005-0.015 to x = 0.049-0.069, a factor
     4.4-9.2. That is real and it matters: the exclusion softens from
     6.5-42.8 sigma to 1.7-8.7 sigma. My previous claim of a clean
     falsification was TOO STRONG and is corrected here.
  2. BUT IT DOES NOT CLOSE THE PROBLEM. The constraint survives at low redshift --
     8.7 sigma at z=2.30 and 6.6 sigma at z=2.85 -- and only dies at z=3.7
     (1.7 sigma). The low-z cutoffs are the tighter measurements (24 and 22 km/s
     against a 12.85 km/s thermal floor), so the surviving constraint rests on the
     best-measured end of the sequence, not the weakest.
  3. THE UV RISK I FLAGGED DID NOT MATERIALISE, and that is why the answer is trustworthy:
     93% of INT P dk comes from k < 1 Mpc^-1, integrand peaking at lambda ~ 130 Mpc. So
     <g^2> is a LARGE-SCALE quantity, determined rather than cutoff-dependent. Diffuse gas
     genuinely is not rescued by unresolved small-scale structure -- the acceleration field
     is not built from small scales.
  4. IT CORRECTS THE REASONING BEHIND MY CHANNEL-A FIX. I said matter 'feels its own halo'.
     For BOUND matter that is right -- but because it is bound and has a large LOCAL
     acceleration, NOT because the cosmological field is small-scale dominated (S3 shows it
     is not). Forest gas is unbound, so gets no such boost. I had conflated two different
     physical cases; the conclusion held for the wrong reason.
  5. VALIDATED BEFORE USE: v_rms(1D, z=0) = 310 km/s vs the observed ~300 km/s. My first
     validation compared a 3D rms against a 1D figure -- a target error, not a code error.

  ONE DIRECTION LEFT UNPRICED, and it runs the framework's way: the rms is a VOLUME average
  over random positions, while forest absorbers sit at mildly OVERDENSE positions where the
  local acceleration is higher than the global rms. That pushes x up and weakens the
  constraint further. Quantifying it needs the conditional acceleration distribution at
  delta ~ 1-10, not computed here. With z=3.35 already at 3.2 sigma, a further modest boost
  could pull the whole high-z half below significance.

  STATUS, REVISED DOWN: the diffuse-sector constraint is REAL BUT NARROWER THAN I CLAIMED --
  carried by z ~ 2.3-2.9 at 6.6-8.7 sigma rather than 7-43 sigma across the board,
  and still subject to the unresolved LCDM-hydro-calibration caveat. Enough to keep the
  missing regulator MANDATORY; not enough to call the pointwise reading falsified.
====================================================================================================
